  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nNo significant changes with the weather forecast, with the heat \nbeing the main story. A large ridge parked over the region will keep \nthings quite hot through Friday. Temperatures this afternoon and \ntomorrow will be at least flirting with, and possibly reaching or \nexceeding, record highs both afternoons. Friday will still be quite \nhot, but may be just a bit further off the slightly higher daily \nrecords. Heat index values will approach or exceed 100 over nearly \nall of our area outside of the higher elevations, with many \nlocations especially south and central will have heat index values \ntop out at or above 105. A heat advisory is in effect for the \nsouthern valley today and this will be extended until Friday \nevening. Will also add a heat advisory for portions of the central \nvalley and northern Plateau for Thu afternoon until Friday evening. \n\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge                \n09-02   100(2011)      98(2011)       96(1943)       99(1953)       \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n\nBy this weekend the upper ridge axis will shift to our west as a \ntrough deepens over the northeastern CONUS with a return to more \nnorthwesterly flow aloft, taking the edge off of the oppressive heat \nas we head through the weekend into early next week. While we will \nback off of the records it will still be hot, and we will also see \nan uptick in isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms even \noutside of the higher terrain especially Saturday through Monday.  \n\nModels generally show a weak surface boundary sagging south toward \nus Friday and then across our area Saturday. CAPE will increase with \nsome steepening of the mid level lapse rates Friday afternoon north \nand then across the remainder of our area Saturday ahead of the weak \nboundary, with significant DCAPE likely to be present as well. Shear \nshould remain weak, but a few storms may become strong to severe \nwith damaging wind gusts the primary threat Friday afternoon/evening \nmainly north and then including the remainder of our area Saturday. \n\nThe ridge is forecast to build back over the region for Tuesday and \nWednesday of next week, but there is some uncertainty in the models \non the strength and placement of the ridge. The ensembles paint \nslightly different pictures, with the EPS/EPS-AIFS camp showing a \nstronger ridge over us which would likely bring a return to very hot \nconditions, and the GEFS camp showing a more muted ridge. Either way \nit will remain hot, but we will have to wait to see if the more \noppressive heat will be returning next week.",
